# rules: csharp-ldap-filter-from-request
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-90.cs
# from: CWE-90 (LDAP Injection)
#
# The search filter is assembled from something the caller sent. LDAP filter
# syntax has no quoting to break out of - the metacharacters are `(`, `)`, `*`,
# `\` and `&`, and they are structure wherever they appear - so a `cn` of `*`
# returns every user, and `*)(objectClass=*` turns a filter for one account
# into a filter for the directory.
#
# It is a smaller rule than the SQL one because the surface is smaller: the
# filter is either the `Filter` property or the second argument to the
# searcher's constructor, and there is nothing else in System.DirectoryServices
# that takes one. Both spellings appear about equally often.
#
# There is no sanitizer, and the reason is worth writing down rather than
# leaving as an empty list. .NET ships no LDAP escaper - no
# `Ldap.Escape(value)` anywhere in the framework - so every correct fix is
# somebody's own helper under somebody's own name, and a list of those names
# would be a list of the codebases this rule has already been run against.
# What a reviewer needs to check is that the value is escaped at all, which is
# what the finding says.
#
# The parameter source is confined to files that are actually MVC. This is the
# lesson the corpus taught and it was expensive: `public $T $M($$$_, string $P,
# $$$_) { $$$_ }` says "a public method taking a string", which in a controller
# means the request and in a utility class means nothing at all. Run over
# ShareX - a desktop application - the path rule reported twenty-seven
# findings, every one of them a `string filePath` parameter of a file helper.
# A file that imports ASP.NET MVC, or declares a class deriving from something
# called Controller, is the test; a file that does neither has no request in it
# and the parameter half of this rule is switched off there.

["$T $V = $S;"] as $assigned
| ["public $T $M($$$_, string $P, $$$_) { $$$_ }",
   "public $T $M(string $P) { $$$_ }"] as $bound
| ["using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;", "using System.Web.Mvc;",
   "using System.Web.Http;", "using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;"] as $mvcImports
| ["class $C : $B { $$$_ }"] as $classes
| ["$S.Filter = $F;",
   "new DirectorySearcher($E, $F, $$$_)",
   "new DirectorySearcher($F)"] as $sinks
| "Request\\s*\\.\\s*(Query|Form|Headers|Cookies|QueryString|Params|RouteValues)\\b" as $fromRequest
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $assigned + $bound + $mvcImports + $classes + $sinks) as $all
| ( ($all | of($assigned) | where_capture("S"; $fromRequest) | focus("S"))
    + ($all | of($bound) | focus("P")
       | in_files_with( ($all | of($mvcImports))
                        + ($all | of($classes) | where_capture("B"; "Controller")) )) ) as $untrusted
| ($all | of($sinks) | focus("F"))
| reaching($untrusted; [])
| finding("csharp-ldap-filter-from-request";
    "this LDAP filter is assembled from the request, and filter syntax has no "
    + "quoting to break out of: a * where a name was expected matches every "
    + "entry in the directory")
| report
